<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Frederick William I of Prussia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1688">1688</a>–<a href="/years/1740">1740</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Soldier King · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-hohenzollern">House of Hohenzollern</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Frederick William I was King in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> from 1713 to 1740, known as the Soldier King. He cut court spending, reformed the finances and the bureaucracy through the General Directory, and roughly doubled the army to around 80,000 men, one of the largest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> for the size of the population. The army and the full treasury he left allowed his son Frederick II to seize Silesia in 1740.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/frederick-william-i-of-prussia.jpg" alt="Frederick William I of Prussia" width="320" height="437" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Antoine Pesne · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Antoine_Pesne_-_K%C3%B6nig_Friedrich_Wilhelm_I._von_Preu%C3%9Fen_(ca._1733).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Frederick William was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> on 14 August 1688, the son of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-i-of-prussia">Frederick I of Prussia</a> and Princess Sophia Charlotte of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a>. A Huguenot governess, Marthe de Roucoulle, raised him in his first years. When the plague of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-northern-war">Great Northern War</a> devastated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, he and a party at court brought down the leading minister Johann Kasimir Kolbe von Wartenberg after an investigation exposed large-scale embezzlement. The episode made him resent crime, corruption and waste, and it was the first time he took an active part in politics. He married his first cousin Sophia Dorothea of Hanover on 28 November 1706, and they had fourteen children.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>His father had paid heavily for the royal title and enlarged palaces at great expense, which ruined the state's finances. On taking the throne in 1713 Frederick William dismissed his father's Cabinet of Three Counts and reorganized the finances. He raised excise taxes, subjected the nobility to a land tax and dictated Regulations for State Officials, which set out every public servant's duties; a minister who missed a committee meeting lost six months' pay. He encouraged commerce and farming, reclaimed marshes and stored grain in good years to sell in bad ones.</p><p>He expanded the Prussian Army from 38,000 men in 1713 to 80,000 in 1740, so that about one man in twenty-five served. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-i-prince-of-anhalt-dessau">Leopold I, Prince of Anhalt-Dessau</a>, he reformed training, tactics and conscription, introducing the canton system, and the iron ramrod raised the infantry's rate of fire. Peasants bore military obligations, while the middle class paid an annual tax in place of service. In 1717 he sold the Brandenburg Gold Coast to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-west-india-company">Dutch West India Company</a>, preferring to spend on the army. In 1732 he invited Salzburg Protestants to settle in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-prussia">East Prussia</a>, which plague had depopulated in 1709. He joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-northern-war">Great Northern War</a> briefly, in alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter the Great</a>, and gained part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Swedish Pomerania</a> with new Baltic ports.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Frederick William had a short temper and sometimes struck servants and even his own children with a cane, and a hereditary disease gave him gout and stomach pains. His relations with his eldest surviving son, the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii">Frederick II</a>, were hostile. After the prince tried to flee to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> with his tutor Hans Hermann von Katte, the king had Katte beheaded in front of him. He died in 1740, aged 51, and was interred at the Garrison Church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potsdam">Potsdam</a>. His reforms left his son the most formidable army in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Frederick William I of Prussia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Frederick William I</b> and Frederick II settled colonists, among them Huguenots welcomed since the Edict of Potsdam of 1685, drained the Oderbruch marshes, built canals and promoted silk, textiles and the potato.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>King <b>Frederick William I of Prussia</b>, uninterested in overseas ventures, sold the posts to the Dutch in 1717.</blockquote><a href="/state/brandenburger-gold-coast">Brandenburger Gold Coast</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in City Palace, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.52,13.38&amp;year=1721" aria-label="Berlin on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4850 352 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Frederick William I of Prussia"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5300 622h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5300 622h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.52,13.38&amp;year=1721">What was here in 1721? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
